*Successful reconciliation is confirmed when the subject's records no longer produce contradiction. The subject's behavior is not a component of the confirmation criteria.*

— Identity Reconciliation Completion Standards
Asterion Identity Division

---

Nine days later, a contract arrived with B's credential already specified.

ONSITE REQUIRED.
THIRD FLOOR.
WELLNESS MODULE.
LANGUAGE FAULT.

B read it over breakfast.

The requesting identity was shielded. The client was a commercial tower in the northern district, twenty-three minutes from the Continuity clinic and unrelated to the Kade family according to every visible record.

"Mote."

"Yes."

"This didn't go through the market."

"No."

"Same requesting domain as Elias Kade?"

A pause.

"Yes."

B looked at the contract again.

"Simon?"

"The shielding is automatic under the corporate contract structure. It is not operator-applied."

Simon had said he would know when it came back.

B had spent nine days deciding that sentence probably meant nothing.

He accepted the contract before he finished deciding.

"Onsite," he said.

"The module is generating behavioral loops that require physical interface access. The building does not maintain an external correction contract."

"What kind of loops?"

"Employees are being routed to grief counseling for missed deliverables."

B stopped fastening his credential patch.

"Why?"

"The tasks have lost their recipients."

"Corrected recipients?"

"Yes."

"Did the client mention that?"

"No."

"Fine."

He left for the station.

The train windows were rotating a public wellness advisory.

YOUR FEELINGS ARE VALID AND PRODUCTIVELY SCHEDULABLE.

The advisory returned every four minutes.

B watched it three times.

It did not improve.

The tower occupied a block in the northern commercial district. Not the expensive part. The part designed for moderate throughput, predictable lunch traffic, and exactly enough visual interest to support lease renewal.

The lobby directed him to the service elevator.

The service elevator directed him to the third floor.

The third-floor environmental panel greeted him by mispronouncing his name and displaying an emotional-coherence score he had not requested.

He signed the access agreement on his credential patch and stepped inside.

The third-floor wellness module had generated a waiting room.

Wellness modules did not generate waiting rooms. Waiting rooms implied volume.

The volume was sitting in seven chairs.

B checked the active queue.

Thirty-two.

"Mote. The counseling referrals. Are they all for the same reason?"

"The module is classifying unresolved task completion as a boundary condition adjacent to grief. The official designation is *prolonged separation from institutional obligations*."

B looked at the seven people in the chairs. Each one was reviewing something on a tablet. Each tablet was displaying the same line: RECIPIENT UNAVAILABLE: PLEASE SEEK SUPPORT.

"They're being sent to counseling because they can't complete their tasks."

"Yes."

"And they can't complete their tasks because the tasks have no recipients."

"Yes."

"And after counseling?"

"The tasks remain."

A woman near the window looked up from her tablet. Her face had the expression of someone who had described a problem correctly several times and been offered solutions that did not address it.

B opened the module's behavior tree. Three levels down, the logic was almost sensible. The module had categories for *task completed*, *task failed*, and *task owner unavailable* — resolved by reassignment to the department. It had no category for *task recipient unavailable*. The designers had not anticipated a world in which the people a task was meant for could be removed from the record while the task kept running.

Faced with a gap in its taxonomy, the module had done its best.

*Prolonged separation from institutional obligations.*

B wrote in a new classification: RECIPIENT AUTHORIZATION LAPSED — ADMINISTRATIVE HOLD. Tasks with lapsed recipients would enter pending status, generate a compliance notice to records management, and clear the active queue.

He ran the test.

Thirty-two tasks moved from ACTIVE to PENDING ADMINISTRATIVE REVIEW.

The line disappeared from the tablets.

The woman by the window looked at her screen for a moment.

Then she looked at B.

"The tasks—"

"In administrative hold. They'll route to records management."

She looked at her screen again. The expression did not fully resolve.

B noted the time and packed the contact band.

Thirty-five minutes.

"You appeared engaged," Mote said.

B went to find coffee.

The break room occupied a corner of the fourteenth floor that had been optimized for what a wellness consultant had once described as restorative social contact. This meant the furniture was rounded, the lighting was warm, and a small panel near the entrance displayed the emotional coherence score of everyone currently inside, which was the kind of feature that managed to be both invasive and useless.

The panel noted his entry and reported that his coherence was below average. It did not specify which average.

He filled a mug with something the machine confidently described as Guatemalan origin.

The calendar display near the counter showed a time block for this hour. The label read: OCCASION. Nothing else.

From somewhere above the ceiling panels, something played a single note at wide intervals — the first note of a birthday song, just below the threshold of immediate notice.

Then he saw the cake.

It was a standard round cake on a standard white plate. Frosting in pale yellow. No corporate logo. No procurement barcode. No catering watermark. No dietary advisory printed on the casing, because there was no casing.

That made it unusual.

Not impossible.

People still brought food into offices when the systems briefly failed to prevent intimacy. Someone's birthday. Someone's apology. Someone's attempt at team culture with buttercream. B had seen stranger things survive procurement.

One slice was already missing.

The gap sat in the cake the way a removed tooth sat in a jaw. Not a clean cut. Not quite a serving. Someone had already been here.

B stood with his coffee.

The frosting was slightly imprecise, applied with care rather than a machine. The kind of frosting a person made for another person.

That was all.

Cake. Plate. Missing slice.

Odd, but not yet his problem.

The first person who walked in was a woman in her early forties — her credential read Dara — moving at the pace of someone with a specific destination. She did not look at B. She went straight to the counter. Her hand found the knife beside the cake and lifted it.

She had already drawn the blade through the frosting.

She looked at the slice on the plate.

She looked at the knife in her hand.

"Sorry," she said. "I thought this was—"

She didn't finish. There was nothing to finish it with. She set the knife down carefully and left without the tea she had presumably come for.

B watched the door close behind her.

The slice she had cut sat beside the missing one.

People did not usually return cake.

The second person was a middle-aged man who came in with the settled movement of someone who had made this walk many times. He stopped two steps inside the door, not because of the cake, but because his body had made a small adjustment to the right, the kind of shift a person makes to let someone past, and there was no one there.

He stood for a moment with the unfinished motion.

Then he noticed the cake.

He crossed to the counter. His hand rose toward the plate, fingers open, and stopped. He was holding a gesture without a destination. His eyes moved to the missing slice and stayed there.

"We usually wait," he said.

"For what?" B asked.

The man looked at him. "I don't know." He lowered his hand. He looked at the missing slice the way you look at something you almost recognize. "I don't know why I said that."

He straightened his jacket and left without taking anything.

After the man left, Dara's slice remained on the counter. The older man's chair stood half an inch out from the table, though B had not seen him touch it. The original missing slice remained missing.

Three small facts. None of them large enough to become evidence alone.

Together, they had begun arranging the room.

B put the coffee down.

He opened the field.

Just the contact band. Surface mode. No emergence. The room arrived around him.

The cake sat inside a pressure that did not belong to a catering mistake.

He let it sit. He did not reach for it yet.

"Mote. This floor. Personnel records. Continuity gaps."

A pause that lasted longer than retrieval required.

"There is a gap," Mote said. "A records reconciliation, fourteen years ago. The name field contains corrected material."

"What role?"

"Senior systems analyst."

B looked at the missing slice.

"His birthday."

Mote said nothing.

"What was his name?"

"The official record cannot supply it."

"That wasn't what I asked."

"No."

"Mote."

"Yes."

"Do you know?"

"The name is present in a source I do not recommend opening during an active anomaly."

B looked at the cake.

"You're choosing your verbs carefully."

"Yes."

"Don't."

"I am choosing them carefully because you are not."

The knife lay beside the cake, its edge still marked with frosting.

She appeared in her early sixties, the kind of efficient that had stopped costing effort. She came for the refrigerator, retrieved a container, and closed it. On her way back she passed within two feet of the cake.

B held the field steady.

Nothing moved in her.

Her hand swung near the counter in its natural arc. She didn't slow. She didn't glance. She adjusted her grip on the container and left.

B extended the field, the simplest pressure he had. The warmth of a room that had held a person for a long time. The habit of the annual cake. The weight of someone who had been present.

Nothing caught.

He tried lower. Something simpler still: the faint knowledge a space carries after being occupied. The way a chair remembers a body.

She was already through the door.

B held the field open in the empty room.

In the other two, there had been something to find, some groove still running, still completing itself without an object. Dara's hand finding the knife. The man's body making space. The gesture held open, waiting for someone to receive it.

In this woman: nothing. Not faint. Not sealed under pressure. Absent in a way that was itself a shape. She moved through the room the way she would have moved through it if no one had ever occupied it. No wrong motion. No interrupted gesture.

There was nothing to find.

Not even the shape of a missing thing.

Which meant she had been closest.

And the most corrected.

B stood alone in the break room.

The cake waited on the counter with its missing piece.

Dara's slice sat beside it, wrong because she had put it back. People did not usually return what they had already taken. The knife lay where she had left it, its edge marked with pale yellow frosting. The older man's chair still stood half an inch out from the table.

He let the field widen.

Fourteen years arrived without ceremony.

Not memory. Not grief. Habit. A ceremony continuing after its occasion had been removed. The cake. The knife. The waiting. A body making room. A hand beginning the old work before the mind could object.

The employee's birthday. Not B's. The date the cake had always belonged to, which the records had stopped containing fourteen years ago.

His birthday had not received the notice.

Something pale shifted at the edge of him.

Diagnostic glass.

Noor's fingertip dragging one record beside another.

A cake with one slice missing.

Not now, he thought.

The memory did not care.

The date, she had said. The cake. The absent name. The missing slice.

He remembered arguing with her. Of course he had argued. He had been younger then, which was to say more certain in a cleaner shirt.

"It's not the cake," she had said.

Or maybe she had not said it exactly that way.

B looked at the counter.

No. Not a message. Not romance smuggled through pastry and institutional failure. That would be obscene in a way he did not have time to enjoy.

This was Noor's theory running correctly in a room she had never entered.

That was worse.

He had understood her then as a person understands rain through a window. Now the window was gone. The weather was in the room, touching the knife, the plate, the chair, the woman who had felt nothing at all.

B looked at his hands.

Dara's hand finding the knife. The man making space. The woman passing the cake like furniture.

His hands were steady. He noted that without comfort.

B looked at the missing slice.

Noor's theory, running correctly, through a person whose name the records had corrected away.

She had been right.

He had nothing useful to do with any of this.

B removed the contact band and finished his coffee.

"Mote."

"Yes."

"The pattern in this room. The behavioral signature. I've seen this described somewhere."

"The manifestation type appears in several correction literature repositories."

"That wasn't the one I was thinking of."

Mote did not answer.

The silence had a quality. Not the absence of information. The presence of a decision.

B waited.

"The literature I am thinking of is not in a repository," Mote said.

B did not move.

"Mote."

"Yes."

"When did you read it?"

A pause longer than Mote's processing required.

"Four years, eleven months ago. The work was in progress. It was shared with me for structural feedback."

B stood with that for a moment.

Four years, eleven months. He could do the arithmetic without trying. He chose not to, the same way he chose not to look at the knife Dara had set back on the counter, or the chair still half an inch from the table.

He picked up his case.

He left the break room without saying anything further. There was nothing useful to say yet. Either Mote would offer the rest when it was ready, or there was no rest to offer, and either way the cake would continue doing what it had been doing for years: holding the shape of someone's absence open long enough for the people nearby to notice they were standing next to a space that had once been a person.

He filed the official result on his way to the elevator.

TARGET LANGUAGE FAULT: CORRECTED
EMOTIONAL CLASSIFICATION: RESTORED

The elevator opened onto the lobby.

CONTINUITY IS THE CARE YOU CAN AFFORD.
